Regex Pattern to Extract Correspondence Addresses

To match addresses in the extracted text, we need a regex pattern that:

1. Detects an address-like structure (street, city, "Isle of Man", postal code).


2. Avoids extra descriptions (like "Reason", "Removed from Register").


3. Captures the entire address block.



Here is a Python regex pattern that should work:

import re

# Example extracted text (from image 1)
text_list = [
    "A LIFE LESS PLASTIC\nRemoved from Register on 15 February 2023\nReason: Ceased to exist\n29-31 Athol Street, Douglas, Isle of Man, IM1 1LB",
    "A LITTLE PIECE OF HOPE\nTo raise money...\nBallakilley Close, Port Erin, Isle of Man, IM9 6BU",
    "A W CLAGUE (DECEASED)\nRemoved from Register on 25 June 2024\nReason: Ceased to exist\nDepartment of Education, Sport and Culture, The Slieau Whallian, Foxdale Road, St. Johns, Isle of Man, IM4 3AS",
    "ACORN\nFor the purpose of...\n2 Marina Close, Onchan, Isle of Man, IM3 4BN"
]

# Regex pattern to match the correspondence address
pattern = r"([A-Za-z0-9 ,'-]+\n){1,3}([A-Za-z0-9 ,'-]+, Isle of Man, [A-Z0-9 ]{5,7})"

# Extract addresses
addresses = [match[1] for text in text_list if (match := re.search(pattern, text))]

# Print extracted addresses
print(addresses)


---

Explanation of Regex:

1. ([A-Za-z0-9 ,'-]+\n){1,3}

Matches 1 to 3 lines of potential text before the address (charity name, descriptions).

Allows alphanumeric characters, spaces, commas, hyphens, and apostrophes.



2. ([A-Za-z0-9 ,'-]+, Isle of Man, [A-Z0-9 ]{5,7})

Captures the main address format.

Looks for ", Isle of Man, " (ensures we capture an actual address).

Postal code is matched by [A-Z0-9 ]{5,7}.





